Windows Basics: Processes, Services, Registry, Users and Groups, UAC and Event Logs
1. Processes
A process is a running program. Each process has a name, a PID (process ID), a parent process, an owner (user) and its own memory.
View them in Task Manager (Processes and Details tabs) or with tasklist in Command Prompt, or Get-Process in PowerShell.
Parent-child relationships matter in security. For example, Microsoft Word starting powershell.exe is suspicious, but explorer.exe starting a browser is normal.
Several system processes always run: System, smss.exe, csrss.exe, wininit.exe, services.exe, lsass.exe (handles logons) and many svchost.exe (host for services). Malware often copies these names, so check the file path.
Event ID 4688 (process creation) records new processes when auditing is on.
2. Services
A service is a program that runs in the background, often from startup, without a user logged in (for example the Windows Update service or the Print Spooler).
Manage them with services.msc, sc query, sc create, sc delete, or in Task Manager (Services tab).
Start types: Automatic, Manual, Disabled. Services run under an account such as Local System (very powerful), Local Service or Network Service.
Many services share one svchost.exe process.
Security angle: attackers install services to keep access after a reboot (persistence). Event ID 7045 (System log) and 4697 (Security log) record new service installs.
3. Registry
The registry is a database of settings for Windows, drivers, software and users. It is edited with regedit.
