Run keys, for example HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run, start programs at logon. Malware often adds entries here.
Changing the registry can break the system, so always back up a key before editing it.
4. Users and groups
Local accounts are stored in the SAM database. Domain accounts are stored on a domain controller.
Account types: Administrator (full control), Standard user (everyday tasks) and Guest (very limited).
Groups give rights to many users at once. Important local groups: Administrators, Users, Remote Desktop Users, Backup Operators.
Commands: net user, net user labuser /add, net localgroup Administrators, or the lusrmgr.msc console.
Related Event IDs: 4720 (user created), 4722 (enabled), 4724 (password reset), 4732 (added to a group).
5. User Account Control (UAC)
UAC asks for permission before an action that needs administrator rights. An administrator normally runs with a limited (filtered) token and only gets the full token after approval.
The prompt usually appears on the secure desktop, which stops other programs from clicking Yes on the user's behalf.
Standard users must enter an administrator password. Administrators only click Yes.
Why it matters: it limits what malware can do without the user noticing. Settings range from "Never notify" to "Always notify".
6. The three main Event Viewer logs
Log
What it records
Typical examples
Value for a SOC analyst
Application
Events from installed programs and Windows components.
An app crash, a database error.
Finding crashes, errors and application misuse.
Security
Audit events: logons, account changes, object access, policy changes, log clearing.
4624, 4625, 4720, 4732, 1102.
Most important log for detecting attacks and tracking users.
System
Events from the operating system, drivers and services.
A service starting or stopping, a driver failing, 7045 (service installed).
Spotting new services, shutdowns and hardware problems.


Open it with eventvwr.msc. Log files are saved as .evtx files in C:\Windows\System32\winevt\Logs.
Each event has a level: Information, Warning, Error, Critical, and for security logs Audit Success or Audit Failure.
Use Filter Current Log to filter by Event ID, and Find to search text.
Remember: processes and services show what is running, the registry shows how it starts, users and groups show who has power, UAC limits that power, and the Security log records what people did.


